# The MCP connector's host (remit-mail/remit#1342). Every mode file imports this
# next to routes.caddy. MCP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN is its own origin, and may be a
# different domain from PUBLIC_ORIGIN; compose hands Caddy http://mcp.invalid
# when it is unset, so with the `mcp` profile off no real host reaches it.
#
# Two ways in, because the mode files differ in what their site address is:
#
#   a site     of its own, {$MCP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN}, in the modes that hold a
#              certificate (acme, internal, tailscale). Caddy obtains one for
#              the MCP host the way it does for the main origin.
#   mcp_route  a host match inside the catch-all :80 site, for the modes with
#              no name on the site (off, tunnel). Under tunnel the origin is
#              https at Cloudflare's edge and plain HTTP here, so either scheme
#              matches.
#
# The client address is stated here the same way routes.caddy states it, and
# any copy a client sent is replaced. Authorization and WWW-Authenticate pass
# through untouched, since reverse_proxy strips neither. flush_interval -1
# streams every response as it is written: the MCP transport may answer with
# server-sent events, which a buffering proxy holds until the stream ends.

(mcp_proxy) {
	request_header X-Remit-Client-IP {client_ip}
	reverse_proxy mcp:8080 {
		import upstream
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

(mcp_route) {
	@mcp expression `"{$MCP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN}" in ["http://" + {http.request.hostport}, "https://" + {http.request.hostport}]`
	route @mcp {
		import mcp_proxy
	}
}
